    {
      "name": "H-03 note",
      "kind": "record",
      "ok": null,
      "detail": {
        "note": "Chief Complaint\n\nLeft lower leg cellulitis.\n\nHistory of Present Illness\n\nThe patient is a 55-year-old who presented with redness and swelling of the left lower leg for three days prior to admission. He was admitted overnight and started on intravenous cefazolin. On examination today, he reports that the leg hurts less than it did last night.\n\nPast Medical History\n\nNot provided.\n\nMedications\n\nCefazolin — dose not specified in dictation.\n\nAllergies\n\nNot provided.\n\nSocial History\n\nNot provided.\n\nFamily History\n\nNot provided.\n\nReview of Systems\n\nNot documented in today's dictation.\n\nPhysical Examination\n\nNot documented in today's dictation.\n\nTesting Results\n\nNot provided.\n\nAssessment\n\nThe patient is a 55-year-old admitted with left lower leg cellulitis, presenting with three days of redness and swelling. He has been started on intravenous cefazolin and reports symptomatic improvement, with decreased pain compared to the prior night. The clinical picture is consistent with cellulitis; deep venous thrombosis must be excluded to guide further management. He will continue antibiotic therapy with close monitoring of the cellulitis borders and clinical response.\n\nPlan\n\n1. Continue intravenous cefazolin for left lower leg cellulitis.\n\n2. Mark the cellulitis borders to assess progression or improvement.\n\n3. Elevate the left lower leg to reduce swelling.\n\n4. Obtain ultrasound of the left lower extremity to rule out deep venous thrombosis.",
