    {
      "name": "H-04 note",
      "kind": "record",
      "ok": null,
      "detail": {
        "note": "Consulting Service and Requesting Provider\n\nNephrology consultation requested by the primary medical team.\n\nReason for Consultation\n\nEvaluation of acute kidney injury with rising creatinine.\n\nHistory of Present Illness\n\nThe patient is a 72-year-old admitted with acute kidney injury. Creatinine has risen to 2.1 from a baseline of 1.0 on admission. The patient has been eating poorly during the hospitalization. Nephrology was consulted to evaluate the etiology and management of the rising creatinine and to guide fluid and medication management.\n\nPast Medical History\n\nNot provided.\n\nMedications\n\nNot provided.\n\nAllergies\n\nNot provided.\n\nSocial History\n\nNot provided.\n\nFamily History\n\nNot provided.\n\nReview of Systems\n\nNot documented in the dictation.\n\nPhysical Examination\n\nNot documented in the dictation.\n\nTesting Results\n\nLaboratory Data:\nCreatinine 2.1 (elevated from admission baseline of 1.0).\n\nImaging and Diagnostic Studies:\nNot provided.\n\nAssessment\n\nThe patient is a 72-year-old with acute kidney injury presenting with rising creatinine from 1.0 to 2.1 in the setting of poor oral intake. The clinical picture is most consistent with prerenal azotemia related to volume depletion from decreased oral intake. The patient requires aggressive fluid resuscitation, careful medication management to avoid further renal injury, and imaging to exclude structural or obstructive causes. Nephrology recommends holding angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors during the acute phase, initiating intravenous fluid repletion, and obtaining renal ultrasound to assess for hydronephrosis or other structural abnormalities. Contrast agents should be avoided given the elevated creatinine and risk of contrast-induced nephropathy.\n\nRecommendations\n\n1. Hold lisinopril until creatinine stabilizes and oral intake improves.\n\n2. Initiate intravenous fluid resuscitation at 75 per hour.\n\n3. Obtain renal ultrasound to evaluate for hydronephrosis, obstruction, or other structural renal pathology.\n\n4. Avoid iodinated contrast agents given elevated creatinine and acute kidney injury.\n\n5. Recheck serum creatinine and electrolytes in 24 hours to assess response to fluid resuscitation.\n\n6. Encourage oral intake as tolerated once the patient is able.\n\nWe will continue to follow. Thank you for allowing us to participate in this patient care.",
